.TH tlsca 1 "FUZIX System Utilities" "FUZIX"
.SH NAME
tlsca \- load the certificate authorities, or stop checking
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tlsca
.I file
.br
.B tlsca \-n
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tlsca
loads a bundle of certificate authorities from
.IR file .
From then on, a TLS connection whose certificate does not check out is
refused rather than made.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-n
Go back to not checking.  Connections are still encrypted.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
# tlsca /etc/ca.pem
TLS: 11085 bytes loaded, certificates are CHECKED
# tlsca -n
TLS: certificates are NOT checked
.fi
.SH WHICH AUTHORITIES YOU NEED IS NOT GUESSABLE
The bundle that ships in
.B /etc/ca.pem
carries ten roots covering Let's Encrypt, Google, DigiCert, Amazon,
SSL.com and Sectigo, which between them cover most of the web.  Sites
move between authorities, and one signed by a root that is not in the
file is refused.
.PP
Even the same company can trip this: Cloudflare's 1.1.1.1 chains to
SSL.com's older ECC root, not their 2022 one, and only a bundle carrying
the right
.I certificate
\- not merely the right company \- verifies it.  To find out what a site
uses, from a desktop machine:
.nf

openssl s_client -connect HOST:443 -servername HOST -showcerts

.fi
and add the root it names.
.SH KEEP THE FILE SMALL
Every certificate in the bundle is parsed on each connection, out of the
same memory the packet buffers use.  Nine or ten roots is comfortable;
the full browser set of about 150 is far too big and will not load at
all.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /etc/ca.pem
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tlsget (1),
.BR ntpdate (1),
.BR wifi (1)
